Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Fig. 1. ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0.83, 0.34, 0.11]],
 "The tree of 2 + 3 \[Times] (5 + 2). Reading it node first, then the \
children left to right, gives the prefix sequence add 2 mul 3 add 5 2."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"6fbd8cd6-729f-441c-916c-a480ce978c76"],

Cell["3. Prefix notation", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"b735c190-0e61-48bc-859c-74dbe60f5cbf"],

Cell["\<\
Prefix (Polish) notation writes each node before its children. Every symbol \
has a fixed arity: add, sub, mul, div and pow take two arguments; exp, log, \
sin and cos one; numbers and x none. So prefix needs no parentheses, and \
trees and prefix sequences correspond one to one (Section 2.1 of the paper).\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8e6457e5-880d-47c6-b3e8-ecdfae4f1f90"],

Cell["3.1 Worked by hand: reading a prefix sequence", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"1f80ee1f-f019-4fbd-a73a-bcff3166705a"],

Cell["\<\
Keep a counter of subtrees still owed. It starts at 1. Each symbol fills one \
slot and opens as many as its arity, so the counter changes by arity \[Minus] \
1. For add 2 mul 3 add 5 2 the counter runs 1 \[RightArrow] 2 (add) \
\[RightArrow] 1 (2) \[RightArrow] 2 (mul) \[RightArrow] 1 (3) \[RightArrow] 2 \
(add) \[RightArrow] 1 (5) \[RightArrow] 0 (2). It reaches 0 exactly at the \
last token, so the sequence is one complete tree. It has 7 tokens; the infix \
form 2 + 3 \[Times] ( 5 + 2 ) has 9.\
\>", "Text",
 CellFrame->{{3, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameColor->RGBColor[0.83, 0.34, 0.11],
 Background->RGBColor[
  1, 0.97, 0.92],ExpressionUUID->"2755a982-feea-43f5-932d-8a41cfd6b1ee"],

