Cell["6. Making integration data", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"66545b2c-eec2-4b91-b213-566fe680211a"],

Cell["\<\
Forward (FWD): draw f and ask a computer algebra system for its integral. It \
is slow, and it only contains integrals that system can find. Backward (BWD): \
draw F and differentiate; the pair (F\[Prime], F) is always available and \
fast. Integration by parts (IBP): if the integral of F\[Prime]G is already \
known, the integral of FG\[Prime] follows with no computer algebra system \
(Section 3 of the paper).\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"867ca5c0-1a88-4906-9183-6ca4f7197356"],

Cell["\[Integral] F G\[Prime] dx = F G \[Minus] \[Integral] F\[Prime] G dx", \
"Text",
 TextAlignment->Center,
 FontFamily->"Times",
 FontSize->15,
 FontSlant->"Italic",ExpressionUUID->"659ae646-1f92-4e09-9b67-3cc5f24b0b20"],

Cell["6.1 Worked by hand: integration by parts", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"85d9184f-9eb3-408d-978f-4107e29c44a8"],

Cell["\<\
Take F = x and G = sin x, so F G\[Prime] = x cos x. The known integral needed \
is \[Integral] F\[Prime]G = \[Integral] sin x = \[Minus]cos x. So \[Integral] \
x cos x dx = x sin x \[Minus] (\[Minus]cos x) = x sin x + cos x. Check by \
differentiating: (x sin x + cos x)\[Prime] = sin x + x cos x \[Minus] sin x = \
x cos x.\
\>", "Text",
 CellFrame->{{3, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameColor->RGBColor[0.83, 0.34, 0.11],
 Background->RGBColor[
  1, 0.97, 0.92],ExpressionUUID->"92929ef0-59d3-46e8-98a5-688660a636d6"],

