Cell["\<\
Check: {n, True, True}: n new pairs, all correct, found without Integrate; \
among them \[PlusMinus]x\.b3 sin x, whose integral \[Minus]x\.b3 cos x + 3x\
\.b2 sin x + 6x cos x \[Minus] 6 sin x is the paper\[CloseCurlyQuote]s \
example of a solution that random generation would almost never produce.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"706b4e8f-ada0-4ae6-87c9-f6aa0bef25bd"],

Cell["7. Checking answers instead of trusting them", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"9c5f4d0d-a63a-4476-a758-06748ab244b3"],

Cell["\<\
Nothing stops a decoder from writing an invalid prefix sequence; such \
hypotheses are thrown away. And an integral can be checked by differentiating \
it. So the paper checks every hypothesis in the beam, not only the most \
likely one; \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]beam size 10\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] means \
at least one of the ten was correct (Section 4.4). A constant of integration \
does not matter.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c8e7216a-1a37-48fa-979e-fab9f84b938d"],

