Cell["\<\
1: pow add INT+ 1 x INT+ 2 (Wolfram orders 1 + x with the number first); the \
counter runs 1, 2, 3, 2, 1, 0, the digits counting as part of their integer. \
2: D(1, 3) = D(1, 2) + D(2, 2) = 6 + 16 = 22. 3: the corrected value equals \
the count; the printed one does not. 4: D(2, 2)/D(1, 3) = 16/22 = 8/11. 5: \
\[Integral] x e\:02e3 dx = x e\:02e3 \[Minus] e\:02e3, whose derivative is x \
e\:02e3.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f2d42c64-c75a-4e31-af46-974eae2de8f1"],

Cell["9. Summary", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"8eb26c34-ccbd-4e14-8ffd-1cc56f70fee2"],

Cell["\<\
Mathematics becomes data in three steps. Expressions are written as trees in \
prefix notation, which is short, needs no parentheses and can be read back \
with a single counter. Trees are sampled uniformly by counting how many ways \
each choice can be completed. Correct pairs come from differentiating (BWD) \
and from integration by parts (IBP), with no computer algebra system. Because \
an answer can be checked, the model need not be trusted: every hypothesis in \
the beam is verified. Counting the trees also caught a slip in the paper\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s Equation 2.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7f9cc01c-11a7-4536-93f2-feb6e52263d8"],

Cell["\<\
Next: Unit 3 builds the transformer that learns from such data, and Unit 4 \
uses all of this on \:2a02/\:2a01 expressions.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"05a102fb-56e6-4de5-a4db-616ed2b4c878"],

Cell["\[Copyright] 2026 Computational Classnotes \[CenterDot] CC BY-NC-SA \
4.0", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"03224111-4b5d-4ffc-90dc-58b88a36acff"]
},
